---
import FaqLanding from '../components/FaqLanding.astro';

const faqs = [
	{
		q: 'What is the Data Privacy Act, in one sentence?',
		a: '<strong>It is the law that makes your business responsible for the personal data it holds.</strong> RA 10173, the Data Privacy Act of 2012, requires you to protect that data, use it only for the purposes you declared, and answer to the National Privacy Commission (NPC) if you don’t.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Do we even need a Data Protection Officer?',
		a: '<strong>Yes. Every organization that handles personal data must designate one, whatever its size.</strong> There is no small-business exemption (IRR Section 26(a); NPC Advisory 2017-01). Size only decides a second question: whether you must also register your data processing systems with the NPC. If you fall below the registration thresholds, you still appoint a DPO and file a notarized sworn declaration instead. A short assessment settles which side you are on.',
	},
	{
		q: 'What does a DPO actually do?',
		a: '<strong>The DPO is the person accountable for your compliance.</strong> They build your privacy program, register you with the NPC, run your breach response, train your staff, and are your official contact with the NPC. The role can be filled in-house or by an external DPO under contract. The NPC allows one outside DPO to serve several companies, and no NPC approval is needed for that.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Do we have to register with the NPC?',
		a: '<strong>Only if one of four grounds applies to you</strong> (NPC Circular 2022-04, Section 5): you employ 250 or more people; you hold sensitive personal information, such as government IDs or health records, of 1,000 or more individuals; your processing is likely to pose a risk to people’s rights; or you use automated decision-making or profiling, which has no threshold. Count your customer database and every employee file together, and most established businesses cross 1,000 sooner than they expect. If no ground applies, you file a notarized sworn declaration of exemption. Either way there is a filing to make.',
	},
	{
		q: 'What happens if we just ignore this?',
		a: '<strong>The NPC can fine you, and it is enforcing.</strong> Failing to register costs ₱50,000 to ₱200,000 per infraction. A grave infraction costs 0.5% to 3% of your prior-year gross income, capped at ₱5 million for any single act. The gravest violations also carry criminal liability that falls on responsible officers personally. In September 2026 the NPC issued show cause orders to Meta, Roblox, Reddit and Discord for failing to register. Compliance costs far less than enforcement.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Our business is small. Is this overkill?',
		a: '<strong>No. Compliance scales with your size.</strong> A small clinic, lending office or hotel does not need an enterprise program. It needs the basics done properly: a lawful basis for each use of data, privacy notices, security measures, a retention schedule, and a breach plan. That is what a right-sized engagement delivers.',
	},
	{
		q: 'What does working with you look like?',
		a: '<strong>It starts with a free assessment.</strong> It tells you whether you need to register, what is missing, and what fixing it would take. From there I can serve as your external DPO (TÜV Certified, Cert #7000532), build your Privacy Management Program, and keep you compliant on retainer. Based in Dulag, Leyte, serving Leyte, Samar and Biliran.',
	},
];
---

